      children: "The battlefield still waits. Arjuna, now asking not as a weeping warrior but as a puzzled student, puts the question that has been gathering since Chapter 3: Krishna, you keep praising renunciation — and you also keep praising the yoga of action. Which is it? The question is the hinge of the whole Gita: will this teaching tell us to leave the world or to live in it? Krishna's reply, in the Mahabharata context of a king's son who must rule and fight, is inevitable: renunciation as a physical act is not possible for you, Arjuna, and not desirable. What is possible — and what outshines even the renunciate's cave — is the inner renunciation of the claim, performed while the hands are at work."
